<p>An online store produces more data than almost any other kind of small business. Every visit, click, cart, discount code and return leaves a trail. The problem is rarely a shortage of numbers. It is knowing which few numbers matter, how they connect, and what to do when one moves.</p>

<p>Many store owners watch revenue and little else. Revenue is the headline, but it hides the story. A store can grow revenue while losing money on every order, because ad costs rose, margins shrank or returns piled up. Business analytics for ecommerce is about connecting the dots: where customers come from, what they buy, what it costs to win them, and whether they come back.</p>

<p>This guide covers the 10 KPIs that give you that complete picture. For each one you will get the formula, a worked example, and the action it should trigger. Prices and examples are in US dollars, and everything applies equally to stores selling in the US and Canada.</p>

<h2 id="why">Why ecommerce analytics is different</h2>
<p>Three things make ecommerce measurement special. First, you can see the whole journey, from the ad click to the checkout, so you can measure every step. Second, margins are often thin, and small percentage changes in conversion, returns or ad cost decide whether you make money. Third, acquiring customers is expensive, so what happens after the first order, meaning repeat purchases, often matters more than the first sale.</p>

<p>That is why the strongest store dashboards combine three groups of numbers: <strong>sales</strong> (what is selling), <strong>marketing</strong> (what it costs to get customers) and <strong>customers</strong> (whether they stay). If you only watch one group, you will misread the others. We cover the marketing side in depth in <a href="/blog/the-real-cost-of-not-tracking-your-marketing-funnel">the real cost of not tracking your marketing funnel</a>.</p>

